# Start MongoDB and run npm start in the terminal first.
# In VS Code, click Send Request above each request, in numbered order.
# Responses open in a separate panel. Expected status codes are noted below.
# Request 2 saves its response so later requests can reuse the new subscriber ID.
# To repeat the demo after deleting, run request 2 again to create a new subscriber.
@baseUrl = http://localhost:5001
@subscriberId = {{createSubscriber.response.body.$._id}}

### 1. List all subscribers (200 OK; [] when empty)
GET {{baseUrl}}/subscribers

### 2. Create a subscriber (201 Created)
# @name createSubscriber
POST {{baseUrl}}/subscribers
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Demo Subscriber",
  "subscribedToChannel": "Web Dev Simplified"
}

### 3. Get the created subscriber by ID (200 OK)
GET {{baseUrl}}/subscribers/{{subscriberId}}

### 4. Edit the subscriber (200 OK; this API uses PATCH, not PUT)
PATCH {{baseUrl}}/subscribers/{{subscriberId}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Updated Demo Subscriber",
  "subscribedToChannel": "Software Architecture"
}

### 5. Read again to verify the changes (200 OK)
GET {{baseUrl}}/subscribers/{{subscriberId}}

### 6. Delete the demo subscriber (200 OK)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/subscribers/{{subscriberId}}

### 7. Verify deletion (404 Not Found)
GET {{baseUrl}}/subscribers/{{subscriberId}}

### 8. Test validation: missing required channel (400 Bad Request)
POST {{baseUrl}}/subscribers
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "Invalid Subscriber"
}

### 9. Get all subscribers again to verify the list is empty (200 OK; [] when empty)
GET {{baseUrl}}/subscribers